/* Wealth Lab Module 13 · Insurance and Estate Design · 2026 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM13Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m13-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","taxYear":2026,"lessonIds":["L13.S1","L13.S2","L13.S3","L13.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L13.S1","title":"How to Calculate Insurance Needs","subtitle":"Financial risk capacity, human capital, coverage gaps, disability protection and independent underwriting","prerequisites":["L1.S1 wealth equation and liquidity","L7.S3 time value of money","L5.S1 insurance risk transfer","L12.S3 Social Security and survivor income"],"objectives":["Distinguish insurable loss from investment risk and expected loss from worst-case loss","Calculate household life insurance using needs and present-value income methods","Compare term coverage, existing assets, survivor income and insurance duration","Calculate insurance affordability against cash-flow and debt stress","Estimate disability income gaps, benefit caps and elimination periods","Evaluate underwriting, insurer claims-paying capacity, exclusions and policy ownership","Write an unbiased coverage recommendation rather than a product pitch"],"sections":[{"title":"What insurance actually purchases","body":["Insurance is a contract in which a premium transfers specified contingent financial consequences to an insurer, subject to definitions, exclusions, limits and claims-paying ability. Risk pooling allows uncertain severe losses to be funded collectively. A life policy pays if its insured death condition is satisfied; it is not a guaranteed investment profit in the ordinary household portfolio sense. Disability coverage has its own definition of inability to work and cannot be inferred from life insurance.","Expected loss is probability multiplied by loss severity, but a household often cares about the catastrophic tail of a rare event, not merely its actuarial average. A family that cannot fund five years of living costs after a breadwinner dies can rationally buy coverage even though expected premiums exceed expected payout under insurer pricing. Premiums also pay administration, reserves, distribution and profit.","Risk management should first identify the exposure and existing controls: savings, other income, employer benefits, Social Security survivor benefits, pensions, public assistance, family support and accessible credit. Buying a product without checking the exposure can cause overlapping coverage, unnecessary expenses or dangerous underinsurance. State the insured event, required payout, duration and household capacity before selecting a policy."],"worked":["Hypothetical 0.2% annual insured-event probability and $500,000 loss imply expected annual loss $1,000.","A $1,300 annual premium may still be rational to transfer a loss the household cannot bear.","Premium minus expected loss is not automatically 'wasted money': claims pooling and risk protection have value.","No numerical expected-loss estimate replaces an insurer's actual underwriting quote."],"practice":[["Why could a household rationally buy insurance with expected payout less than total expected premiums?","Catastrophic financial loss can threaten basic housing or dependent care; transferring tail risk is valuable even with administrative loads."],["Is cash-value growth the primary analytical definition of a life insurance need?","No. Begin with the effect of the insured person's death on survivors' obligations and resources; cash value is a separate feature."]]},{"title":"A detailed death-benefit needs worksheet","body":["The needs method inventories debts that the survivor would reasonably need to discharge, living-support needs, education, childcare and special-care obligations, final expenses and a transition reserve. It then subtracts genuinely available resources such as liquid assets, existing transferable life insurance, pensions and realistic survivor support. The result is a funding gap rather than an instruction to insure every outstanding dollar regardless of whether debts should be paid.","Suppose a household has a $220,000 mortgage, $250,000 target for survivor support, $30,000 education need, $10,000 final-cost estimate and $40,000 accessible savings. The basic needs result is $470,000. The model must not count the home value as immediate liquid money while simultaneously assuming the family will remain in that home. Nor should it assume an unvested workplace death benefit is portable.","Coverage sizing depends on dependents' ages and needs. A child with twenty years until financial independence creates a different horizon from an independent adult. The household can select level-term protection for a period, consider decreasing needs over time, or create layered policies. Every recommendation records contract definitions, premium affordability and whether survivor needs persist after the term expires."],"worked":["Mortgage $220,000 + income support target $250,000 + education $30,000 + final expenses $10,000 = $510,000.","Less usable resources $40,000 = estimated $470,000 uncovered need.","If an existing portable $100,000 policy is confirmed and not otherwise allocated, modeled new gap could fall to $370,000.","Recalculate if surviving spouse continues the mortgage instead of paying it off; this changes assumptions, not facts."],"practice":[["Can a family count full home equity as emergency cash while also expecting to keep living in the same home?","No. Doing so double-uses the home; sale, refinancing or borrowing can be expensive or infeasible after death."],["Why is the worksheet result not an automatic life insurance quote?","It omits underwriting, coverage term, survivor benefits, income changes and insurer policy restrictions."]]},{"title":"Human-capital present value and replacement horizon","body":["Human capital represents the economic value of a person's future contributions to household finances and care. A rigorous life insurance model can discount the net contribution the household loses each year, rather than equating a worker's full pretax wage with the amount survivors need. Subtract the decedent's personal consumption, payroll taxes and costs that disappear, then add household services and childcare replacement that would now require money.","Suppose a person contributes $30,000 per year to household living support for twenty years and the relevant real discount rate is 3%. The value of a constant real annuity is $30,000×[1−(1.03)^−20]/0.03, approximately $446,324. Earnings growth, tax changes, inflation and death timing are deliberately simplified. A replacement-value calculation should be reconciled to the needs worksheet; adding both full estimates without considering overlap double-counts survivor support.","Discount rates are not the advertised investment returns from a permanent life illustration. A realistic analyst stress-tests zero, moderate and higher real rates, duration of dependents' needs and survivor ability to work. Coverage need can decline over time as debts fall and savings grow, yet a permanent need can remain for a disabled dependent or estate liquidity objective."],"worked":["Annual real net support contribution $30,000 for 20 years.","Real discount rate assumed 3%; annuity factor [1−(1.03)^−20]/0.03 ≈14.8775.","Human-capital present value ≈$446,325, subject to rounding.","Combine with other debts and resources without counting the same living-support obligation twice."],"practice":[["Why discount future lost support rather than adding twenty undiscounted annual amounts?","Money needed years later has a time value; a present reserve can potentially earn returns while supporting future obligations, though rates are uncertain."],["Why is the gross salary often too high for a replacement-needs calculation?","Not all gross earnings would have been spent on survivors; personal consumption, taxes and changing costs must be accounted for."]]},{"title":"Term policy duration, layering and the portability trap","body":["Term life protection offers a specified death benefit for a contractual period, typically without an accessible cash-value account. Premiums generally depend on underwriting, age, health, coverage and policy type. A level-premium term can be useful for a mortgage and dependent-income need that ends at a foreseeable date. Renewal and conversion rights may differ dramatically between two policies both described as 'term life'.","Employer-provided coverage can be convenient and sometimes subsidized. But a person changing jobs, becoming disabled or losing employer eligibility may discover that group coverage ended or conversion prices are high. A comparison should ask whether coverage can be continued independently, whether amounts are reduced at certain ages and whether accelerated-death or waiver-of-premium riders change the terms.","Layering can match different needs to different periods—for example a 20-year $300,000 income-replacement layer and a 10-year $150,000 debt layer. The combined first-decade death benefit is $450,000, falling to $300,000 in years 11–20. This can be more affordable than keeping unnecessary maximum coverage for 30 years, but every quoted policy and beneficiary must be checked."],"worked":["20-year level term layer $300,000 plus 10-year additional layer $150,000.","Years 1–10 total $450,000 insured death benefit if both contracts remain active.","Years 11–20 total $300,000 if first policy remains active and second expires.","After year 20 coverage from these two contracts is $0 unless renewed or replaced."],"practice":[["Why might an employer-paid group policy be insufficient by itself?","Coverage can end or change with job status, and portability, conversion and guaranteed terms vary by employer contract."],["Does the phrase '20-year term' mean guaranteed affordable renewal into retirement?","No. Check expiration, premiums, renewal guarantees, conversion deadlines and policy documents."]]},{"title":"Disability risk may be more immediate than death risk","body":["Disability insurance can protect earnings while the insured is alive but cannot work. Contract language is decisive: own-occupation coverage can differ from any-occupation terms, partial/residual disability, benefit periods, mental-health limitations, exclusions and the requirement to document continuing impairment. Social Security Disability Insurance has its own eligibility rules and delays; it should not be treated as a seamless substitute for private coverage.","Suppose a worker's take-home pay is $3,200 per month, essential expenses are $2,700, and a disability policy is expected to provide $1,900 monthly after a 90-day elimination period. The essential gap after benefit begins is $800 monthly, before considering taxation of disability benefits or other resources. The first three months may require $8,100 of essential funding if no other income arrives; that exceeds a $6,000 emergency reserve.","Benefit taxation depends partly on whether premiums were employer-paid or after-tax employee-paid and how coverage was structured. Check offsets for workers' compensation, SSDI and other benefits. A policy that advertises '60% of income' may have a monthly dollar cap, taxable benefit and a waiting period, so the true household cash-flow benefit could be less than the headline rate."],"worked":["Essential outflow $2,700/month, 90-day elimination period approximated as three months.","Unfunded essential wait-period need $2,700×3=$8,100.","After a hypothetical $1,900/month disability benefit starts, essential gap $800/month.","A $6,000 cash reserve leaves $2,100 short for the wait period without other aid."],"practice":[["Why should an income-replacement quote disclose an elimination period?","Benefits often begin only after a waiting period, requiring the household to self-fund the initial expenses."],["Is '60% of income' guaranteed to equal 60% of after-tax wages received?","No. Benefit caps, offsets, exclusions and tax treatment can alter what is actually payable."]]},{"title":"Insurance economics: premiums versus saving the difference","body":["An honest comparison of term and permanent coverage begins by holding the death-benefit need and the household's budget constant. A policy that includes cash value generally requires a different premium commitment than term insurance. The incremental cost can be considered alongside separate IRA, workplace plan or brokerage investing. It is misleading to compare only the accumulated cash value of a high-premium policy against a low-premium term policy while pretending the premium difference disappears.","Suppose two hypothetical contracts cover the same death risk, a term premium is $35/month and a permanent policy premium is $250/month. The monthly cost difference is $215. At an assumed 6% annual nominal return compounded monthly for 20 years, investing $215 at each month end could accumulate roughly $99,300 before taxes, investment fees and market volatility. Real returns can be negative; this model is not proof that separate investing always wins.","A permanent policy can provide benefits not replicated by a simple term-plus-brokerage strategy, such as lifetime death coverage, particular guarantees and insurance-specific planning features. But they must be weighed against surrender charges, funding strain, liquidity restrictions and the ability to keep premiums current. The appropriate recommendation comes from side-by-side guaranteed and non-guaranteed illustrations, not slogans."],"worked":["Term premium $35/month versus permanent premium $250/month => difference $215/month.","Monthly investment rate hypothetical 6%/12=0.5%.","Future value for 240 monthly end contributions $215×[(1.005^240−1)/0.005]≈$99,300.","Term death benefit still needs to be considered; separate portfolio capital is not a guaranteed insurer benefit."],"practice":[["Why is comparing policy cash values without comparing premiums unfair?","Different premium outlays create different funding costs. Equal-budget analysis must account for every cash dollar diverted or invested."],["Does a hypothetical 6% account return make investing the difference risk-free?","No. Returns fluctuate, fees and taxes matter, and a brokerage account does not guarantee an immediate death benefit."]]},{"title":"Contract underwriting, exclusions and insurer solvency","body":["Life and disability insurance underwriting can consider age, health, occupation, coverage size and other legally permitted factors. A quoted premium is not a binding contract until applied, underwritten and accepted. Misstatements and contestability periods may affect claims. Financial-strength ratings help examine insurer ability to pay but cannot prove a policy is risk-free; state guaranty associations have limitations and are not substitutes for due diligence.","Request the policy schedule, application, rider definitions, grace-period terms, exclusions, conversion conditions and claims process. For permanent policies, collect guaranteed cash values and current non-guaranteed illustration columns; for disability policies, get benefit caps, renewability, elimination period and a written summary of occupation standards. Sales representations made orally are not a substitute for the contract.","Policy replacement is a separate risk. Surrendering an older contract may forfeit guarantees, create taxable gain or restart exclusions or suicide/contestability provisions in a new policy. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners recommends careful comparison and not canceling existing coverage before a replacement is approved and operational. Document the costs and alternatives."],"worked":["Coverage $500,000 at hypothetical $50/month costs $600 annually; two decades of premiums $12,000 without renewal changes.","A second quote of $80/month costs $960 annually; price difference $360/year.","The cheaper quote may exclude desirable riders or have different conversion and underwriting terms.","Rate and cost comparisons are valid only after confirming comparable benefit triggers and financial strength."],"practice":[["What is the danger of canceling a current policy before a new one is active?","The applicant can be rejected or receive new restrictions, leaving the family uninsured and losing existing guarantees."],["Does a high insurer rating make every proposed contract a good fit?","No. Contract benefits, restrictions, pricing and household needs still determine suitability."]]},{"title":"From risk audit to household protection recommendation","body":["A university-level protection recommendation documents assets, liabilities, beneficiaries, household dependents, future incomes, health coverage, disability risks, insurance contracts and alternative protection methods. It explicitly identifies any double counting between the income-replacement method and debt-payoff method. A recommendation must state what risks the household chooses to retain because unlimited insurance would be unaffordable.","Use a hypothetical Connecticut family pursuing homeownership with $250–$500 available monthly. Protect essential bills and short-term housing reserves before funding any long-premium commitment. Compare at least three outcomes: affordable term coverage with separate savings, smaller coverage with increased liquid reserve and permanent coverage requiring rigorous guaranteed-value review. The analysis does not presume a permanent policy should be purchased merely because a loan might one day be available.","Finish with implementation gates: written underwriting quote, full insurer illustration, independent fee/premium comparison, documented beneficiary designations, automatic premium affordability, and annual review after changes in health, dependents or mortgage size. Financial literacy means the household can explain what protection it is buying and what is not covered."],"worked":["Needs: mortgage $220k + support $250k + education $30k + final costs $10k−assets $40k = $470k.","Affordable term policy might target a rounded $450k–$500k subject to actual underwriting, with uncovered deviations documented.","A permanent premium exceeding household saving capacity can increase lapse risk and reduce mortgage-funding liquidity.","Protection recommendation includes a decision log and signed plan to revisit beneficiaries each year."],"practice":[["Why can a policy with attractive projected cash value still be unsuitable?","Premium obligations may exceed safe monthly surplus, trigger lapse risk and crowd out protected bills or a home-purchase reserve."],["Which five items should accompany a recommendation?","Need calculation, timeline, comparable contracts, premium sustainability, beneficiaries and renewal/review checkpoints."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["NAIC choosing life insurance and policy needs","https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-what-type-life-insurance-right-you"],["NAIC insurance illustrations and guarantees","https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/life-insurance-illustrations"],["IRS death benefit taxation","https://www.irs.gov/faqs/interest-dividends-other-types-of-income/life-insurance-disability-insurance-proceeds/life-insurance-disability-insurance-proceeds"],["Investor.gov financial planning and asset allocation","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["SSA disability insurance eligibility","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/"],["Connecticut Insurance Department consumer insurance assistance","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-affairs/consumer-affairs"],["IRS disability benefit taxation","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"],["NAIC life insurance consumer overview","https://content.naic.org/consumer/life-insurance"]],"assessment":{"title":"Fiduciary-style household protection study","prompt":"Analyze a hypothetical household with $220k mortgage, $250k survivor support need, $30k education, $10k final expense and $40k genuinely accessible assets. Model $30k constant real annual contributions for 20 years at 3% real discount rate without double counting needs. Analyze a three-month disability elimination period for $2,700 monthly essentials and a $1,900 thereafter benefit. Compare $35/month term against $250/month permanent policy and invest the $215/month difference for 20 years under hypothetical 6% nominal rate, with risk and tax caveats. Write a detailed recommendation covering insurer, beneficiary, contract and monthly affordability.","rubric":["Death-benefit needs and double-count prevention (25%)","Present-value analysis and real/nominal assumptions (15%)","Disability and emergency liquidity funding (20%)","Premium tradeoffs with independent protection comparisons (20%)","Documentary evidence, suitability and ethical reporting (20%)"],"answerKey":["Needs-based coverage gap $470,000.","Human-capital support PV $30,000×(1−1.03^−20)/.03≈$446,325; don't add to $250k support need without reconciliation.","Three-month essential elimination gap $8,100; thereafter essential gap $800/month.","$215 monthly invested at 6%/12 for 240 months yields approximately $99k before fees/taxes with investment risk.","Do not recommend a permanent commitment solely to access policy loans; require full insurer illustration, solvency and beneficiary review."]}},{"id":"L13.S2","title":"Whole Life, Universal Life & Policy Loans","subtitle":"Contract mechanics, guaranteed versus illustrated values, loan interest, lapse taxation and trust ownership","prerequisites":["L13.S1 protection need and insurance term design","L7.S3 compounding and present value","L11.S1 ordinary taxable income","L12.S2 tax wrappers and opportunity cost"],"objectives":["Distinguish term, whole life, universal, indexed universal and variable insurance structures","Read guaranteed/non-guaranteed premiums, death benefit, accumulation and surrender-value illustrations","Calculate loan interest, net death proceeds, surrender gains and policy-lapse shortfalls","Explain cost basis, withdrawals, modified endowment contracts and contract taxation limitations","Distinguish policy owner, insured, payor, beneficiary and trustee","Evaluate ILIT control, estate inclusion, three-year lookback and Crummey gifts conceptually","Compare borrowing against insurance cash value with home-funding alternatives"],"sections":[{"title":"Permanent insurance separates insurance coverage from contract value","body":["Whole life insurance typically combines long-duration death benefit protection, contractually scheduled premiums and policy cash-value guarantees as written. A participating whole life contract may credit dividends that are not guaranteed. Universal life separates account-value mechanics from ongoing cost-of-insurance charges, premium flexibility and interest crediting. Indexed universal life credits according to a contract formula linked to an index subject to caps, participation and other adjustments; it is not direct ownership of the index.","Variable life and variable universal contracts expose account value to selected investments and related market risk while their charges continue. A high projected return can make a flexible-premium illustration look comfortable, yet low returns or rising insurance charges can create a funding shortfall. The words 'permanent' or 'lifetime' do not mean premiums may safely stop at any time. Longevity of coverage depends on guarantees and continued compliance.","An insurance policy illustration contains guaranteed and non-guaranteed columns. Compare the same dates, premiums, net death benefit, surrender values, expected charges, loan status and contract year under both scenarios. The premium paid is not the same as deposit credited to accessible cash value: costs of insurance, acquisition expenses and surrender penalties can initially produce far less cash value than cumulative premiums."],"worked":["Hypothetical premiums $250/month for 10 years total $30,000.","Contract may show $18,000 cash surrender value at year 10, not the entire $30,000 paid.","The $12,000 difference is not automatically a single fee; death coverage, charges and non-guaranteed credits affect value.","A favorable illustrated $25,000 account value does not override the guaranteed contractual value."],"practice":[["Is indexed universal life an investment account that owns S&P 500 shares directly?","No. Index crediting is based on contractual formula and limits; the policyholder generally does not hold the index assets."],["Can a level premium guarantee that a flexible universal life policy stays in force indefinitely?","Not unless an applicable enforceable guarantee and all its payment conditions are satisfied; monitor charges and in-force status."]]},{"title":"Cash value, surrender value, basis and effective returns","body":["Account value, cash surrender value and death benefit are different reporting concepts. Account value reflects the policy's crediting and charges; surrender value is the amount available on cancellation after contractual surrender deductions and loan payoff where applicable. Death benefit describes what the carrier owes under the contract's death claim conditions, often reduced by outstanding loans and interest. The beneficiary does not necessarily receive the sum of account value and the separately advertised death benefit.","An after-tax outlay comparison should include all premiums, riders and contract expenses. Simple cash-value divided by total premiums is not a compound return. The mathematically appropriate measure is internal rate of return using dated negative premium cash flows and a final hypothetical surrender or death payout, and including taxation when relevant. A policy can produce a worthwhile insured death benefit before it produces strong surrender liquidity.","Non-guaranteed dividends and illustrations can be revised. Obtain current in-force illustrations reflecting the actual policy ledger. Evaluate surrender against reducing coverage, paid-up options, permitted partial withdrawals, exchange to a suitable replacement and keeping the policy. Never treat the salesperson's future-number column as a certainty or an unrestricted bank deposit."],"worked":["Total premiums paid $30,000; hypothetical cash surrender value $18,000.","Surrender-value-to-premiums ratio 60%, NOT a 60% annual return.","Hypothetical cumulative cash loss on cancellation $12,000 before tax effects.","A $250,000 death benefit at death is a distinct insured contingency, not $250,000 liquid during the owner's lifetime."],"practice":[["Why is 'cash value ÷ premiums' not an internal rate of return?","It ignores timing of cash payments, interim charges, annual compounding and the distinct benefit of death protection."],["Why might death benefits and cash values not both be paid in full to beneficiaries?","Contract structures generally define one net death benefit, including permitted adjustments; additions are policy-specific, not automatically additive."]]},{"title":"A policy loan is real debt against contract value","body":["A policy loan generally permits the owner to borrow under a contract while assigning the policy's cash value as security. The insurer charges interest at a defined fixed or adjustable rate. Outstanding loan principal and accrued interest reduce net benefits or surrender proceeds. Loan availability depends on eligible values and contract language. The borrower has received spendable cash but acquired a corresponding obligation, so borrowing does not generate new net worth.","Borrow $15,000 at a hypothetical 7% simple annual interest and no principal payments: one year's interest is $1,050. Under annual capitalization for five years at 7%, the debt rises to $15,000×1.07^5≈$21,038. The policy might credit interest or dividends on some retained value, but this does not make the loan rate disappear. Direct-recognition and non-direct-recognition dividend provisions can modify economic cost.","When leveraged cash is used for a house, household debt exposure becomes more complex. The mortgage lender may count outstanding obligations or scrutinize the down-payment source. A pledged policy could lapse precisely when premiums, loan interest and household spending are under stress. A clear borrower ledger includes loan balance, annual interest, premium obligations, annual available surrender value and death benefit under adverse illustrated scenarios."],"worked":["Loan principal $15,000, simple first-year rate 7% => $1,050 interest.","If interest compounds annually unpaid for five years, balance = $15,000×1.07^5≈$21,038.","If death benefit face amount is $250,000, simplified net death proceeds might be ≈$228,962 before other changes.","No scenario can assume loan proceeds and full unchanged $250,000 death benefit with zero debt effect."],"practice":[["Does paying policy-loan interest mean the household is 'paying itself' in every policy?","No. Interest generally goes to the insurer under contract; dividend and crediting responses vary but do not erase the obligation."],["How does loan capitalization change risk?","Unpaid interest increases the outstanding loan, potentially reducing net proceeds and raising the chance of policy lapse."]]},{"title":"Lapse and surrender taxation: the hidden leveraged-policy risk","body":["Life insurance surrender can generate taxable ordinary income if proceeds exceed the owner's investment in the contract, calculated under tax rules and adjusting for prior transactions. A policy lapse with an outstanding loan may create a taxable transaction even though the owner receives little or no cash at lapse. Loan proceeds in a non-MEC policy are often not currently taxable when the policy stays in force, but that statement is conditional, not a tax exemption for every outcome.","Consider a simplified policy with $40,000 aggregate premiums/basis, $60,000 surrender value before $35,000 loan payoff and $25,000 cash net received. For tax modeling, the surrender amount economically realized can include debt satisfied from the policy, making the gain $60,000−$40,000=$20,000 notwithstanding only $25,000 of cash received. Actual tax treatment requires the policy's basis, gain and transactions from the insurer. A borrower who borrowed and spent the funds years earlier may face taxable gain without current cash for the bill.","Modified endowment contracts (MECs) have different loan and distribution taxation and may expose certain income-first withdrawals and loans to additional tax when the owner is under age 59½, absent exceptions. MEC status can arise when premium funding exceeds statutory tests, and tax law has special rules for contracts materially changed. Never describe tax-favored policy borrowing without a written MEC classification and ledger."],"worked":["Simplified contract basis $40,000, gross surrender value $60,000, loan satisfied $35,000.","Actual net cash paid to owner $60,000−$35,000=$25,000.","Simplified realized policy gain $60,000−$40,000=$20,000 (typically ordinary taxable income under qualifying facts).","A 22% illustrative federal marginal rate could create ~$4,400 income tax despite only $25,000 net cash, before state effects."],"practice":[["Why can a policy lapse produce income tax even when there is no cash distribution at lapse?","Satisfaction or cancellation of a policy loan against contract value can enter the surrender/distribution accounting, revealing gain above tax basis."],["Can all life insurance loans be treated as tax-free distributions?","No. MEC classification, lapse, surrender, transfer and policy details can materially change tax treatment."]]},{"title":"Loan-to-value stress and premium sustainability","body":["A financially responsible in-force policy stress test projects yearly loan balances, annual premium capacity, credited values, rising insurance costs and potential lapse. It uses the insurer's guaranteed and current assumptions rather than extrapolating one high illustration curve. A contract with a $50,000 surrender value but a growing $40,000 loan has less flexibility than the gross cash-value number suggests.","Suppose loan principal is $30,000, rate 8% and no repayments. In five years the compounded balance is roughly $44,080. If net surrender value under a guaranteed low-earning scenario is $42,000, the loan can threaten policy sustainability before the illustration's original death-benefit horizon. Insurance crediting and loan charges may be different rates and recognition methods, so a simplistic net spread assumption is dangerous.","A homebuyer putting $250–$500 monthly into a new policy for the sole purpose of future borrowing faces another constraint: early-year cash values are frequently below total paid premiums. They could lack sufficient usable collateral when their home purchase arrives. Compare waiting, conventional cash savings, down-payment assistance eligibility and credit profile before locking cash into an illiquid contract."],"worked":["Loan $30,000 at 8% with five annual capitalizations → $30,000×1.08^5≈$44,080.","Illustrative stressed net surrender value $42,000 implies a ~$2,080 shortfall compared with loan.","Loan-to-surrender-value at year 5 ≈105%, unsustainable without corrective funding or other policy mechanisms.","A lender's home down-payment documentation rules remain separate from the insurer's loan eligibility."],"practice":[["Why is five-year policy borrowing not simply principal times 8%?","When interest is added to the loan balance, interest accrues on earlier unpaid interest, so the debt compounds."],["What makes using a new permanent policy as a short-term down-payment account particularly risky?","Initial surrender values can be low and contract charges, loan interest and tax exposure can consume expected borrowing capacity."]]},{"title":"Who owns the policy? insured, owner, payor, beneficiary and trustee","body":["The insured person's death triggers a death benefit, but the owner holds most contractual rights during life: changing designated beneficiaries where allowed, surrendering, borrowing and selecting some riders. The premium payor can be someone else. A beneficiary receives payable death proceeds under the insurer's policy terms. A trustee controls a policy only if legal ownership has actually been transferred to the trust or originally issued to it and the trustee has authority under the trust document.","Changing a beneficiary to a trust does not by itself mean the trust owns the policy. Putting a policy inside a revocable trust commonly leaves the grantor with retained control and may not keep death proceeds out of the taxable estate. Irrevocable life insurance trusts (ILITs) are specialized legal structures that can, when correctly designed, transfer ownership and control but restrict personal use of policy cash value. This undercuts claims that someone can both remove a policy from their estate and freely borrow it for a down payment.","Federal estate inclusion can depend on incidents of ownership and certain transfers within three years of death. A properly structured new policy acquired by an independent irrevocable trust can differ from transferring an existing personally owned policy. Gift-tax contributions, beneficiary notices such as Crummey powers, trustee duties and trust income taxes require individual legal/tax attention."],"worked":["Policy with $300,000 death benefit; individually owned by insured who retains incidents of ownership.","Death proceeds generally may be excluded from beneficiary's federal income but still potentially included in insured's estate for estate-tax accounting.","Designating the trust as beneficiary without transferring policy ownership does not automatically change ownership-based estate inclusion.","A genuine ILIT ownership strategy must account for relinquished owner control and independent trustee fiduciary duties."],"practice":[["If a policy lists an irrevocable trust as beneficiary, does the trust automatically own it?","No. Owner and beneficiary are separate designations. The insurer's ownership record and executed legal transfer control contractual authority."],["Can the insured freely borrow from an ILIT-owned policy as if personally owned?","Not automatically. The trustee controls contract rights under fiduciary and trust terms; personal access can undermine the intended legal and tax strategy."]]},{"title":"MEC, 1035 exchange and policy replacement decisions","body":["A Section 1035 exchange may permit certain exchanges of insurance or annuity contracts without immediate gain recognition if statutory requirements are met. It is not a blanket permission to cash out a policy and deposit the proceeds anywhere, and an outstanding loan can complicate a transaction by causing taxable boot or deemed distribution. Replacing a policy can reset acquisition expenses, alter riders and generate new surrender charges.","MEC rules are governed by tax tests rather than sales terminology; a policy can become a modified endowment contract if funding crosses the seven-pay test under relevant definitions. MEC income-first distribution and loan taxation make aggressive short-period premiums especially important to evaluate. Surrender and replacement costs must be compared against keeping or modifying existing coverage.","A suitable written decision tree asks whether the insured still needs the death benefit, whether current policy is affordable, projected values on a guaranteed basis, how debt is handled, whether the insured qualifies for replacement coverage and how each option is taxed. Obtain an insurer-supplied statement of cost basis and MEC status. Never use a replacement solely because its new illustration displays larger non-guaranteed numbers."],"worked":["Old policy basis $20,000, surrender value $26,000, no other adjustments.","Simple outright taxable gain might be $6,000; a qualifying 1035 exchange may defer recognition subject to rules.","If an outstanding loan is relieved in a replacement, taxable treatment could differ; cannot infer from basis and value alone.","A new first-year surrender schedule can make replacing a policy costly even if a favorable headline premium appears."],"practice":[["Does a 1035 exchange make the entire transaction immune from tax whatever happens to an existing loan?","No. Eligibility, transferred value, loans and nonqualifying cash or other consideration must be analyzed."],["Why should the insurer identify MEC status before discussing tax-favored borrowing?","MEC loans and distributions can be taxable under different rules and may face additional tax."]]},{"title":"A policy illustration due-diligence committee for homebuyers","body":["Build an unbiased comparison with the same initial $250 or $500 monthly budget: pure term protection plus high-liquidity reserve, permanent insurance plus verified contract values, and employer coverage supplemented with independently owned term. Model five, ten and twenty-year horizons and compare both expected death claims and accessible living capital. Do not call one option the 'winner' before documenting the specific household risk tolerance and dependent-protection need.","Suppose a hypothetical permanent policy requires $400 monthly, leaving $100 from a $500 available savings budget for house reserves. If the home down-payment target is $15,000 within four years, only saving $100 monthly accumulates $4,800 before interest, far below target. The insurance loan feature is irrelevant if contract cash value and lending terms will not reliably fund the difference. Conversely, term coverage may preserve a high death benefit while separate cash accumulates for closing.","The memo must label guaranteed and non-guaranteed policy value scenarios, lapse stress, loan capitalization, net death payout, basis and surrender tax. It must also state the irreducible tradeoff: transferring a policy to an irrevocable trust to remove control is usually incompatible with treating its cash value as an unrestricted personal checking account. Request an independent insurance professional, estate attorney and tax review for complex arrangements."],"worked":["$500/month available; hypothetical permanent premium $400/month, leaves $100/month for housing savings.","Four years (48 months) with no return, $100/month saves $4,800.","Target $15,000 leaves $10,200 unfunded before other resources.","Even if a policy advertises a projected loan, it cannot be counted as guaranteed down-payment cash until in-force terms and lender requirements verify it."],"practice":[["Why is a 2026 policy-loan illustration insufficient proof of future down-payment availability?","Future contract value, surrender deductions, loan eligibility, terms, insurer behavior and lender source-of-funds rules may differ from the illustration."],["What is the main control tradeoff of using an ILIT to hold a policy?","The trust's proper owner/trustee controls rights according to its terms, which can limit the insured's direct access to policy cash value."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["NAIC policy illustration guarantees and nonguarantees","https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/life-insurance-illustrations"],["NAIC how to choose life insurance","https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-what-type-life-insurance-right-you"],["IRS Publication 525 surrender and life proceeds","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"],["IRS life insurance beneficiary income taxation","https://www.irs.gov/faqs/interest-dividends-other-types-of-income/life-insurance-disability-insurance-proceeds/life-insurance-disability-insurance-proceeds"],["IRS Section 1035 and insurance contract exchanges","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p575"],["IRS estate tax ownership and life insurance inclusion","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i706"],["US Code 26 USC 7702A modified endowment contracts","https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title26-section7702A"],["CT Department of Insurance insurance consumer resources","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-affairs/consumer-affairs"]],"assessment":{"title":"Permanent insurance and policy leverage review","prompt":"Compare term coverage to a 20-year permanent-policy illustration: identify guaranteed versus projected values, premium outlay, death benefits and beneficiary access. Compute $15,000 policy loan at 7% over five years, simple year-one interest and capitalized ending debt; model a surrender with $40,000 basis, $60,000 gross contract value and $35,000 unpaid loan. Stress a $30,000 loan at 8% for five years against $42,000 stressed surrender value. Distinguish ownership/beneficiary/trust roles and present a home-down-payment recommendation within a $500 monthly cap.","rubric":["Contract tax/crediting distinctions and insurer guarantees (20%)","Loan balance and lapse stress math (25%)","Basis, surrender and MEC tax reasoning (20%)","ILIT owner control and beneficiary analysis (20%)","Independent suitability and home liquidity judgment (15%)"],"answerKey":["$15,000 at 7% simple annual interest owes $1,050 interest year one; five years annual capitalization yields about $21,038.","Surrender $60,000 gross, $35,000 loan payoff, $25,000 cash net, $40,000 basis => illustrative taxable gain $20,000.","$30,000×1.08^5≈$44,080 loan versus $42,000 net surrender estimate creates $2,080 risk shortfall.","ILIT ownership requires actual owner control by trustee and potentially restricts borrower access; beneficiary designation alone does not transfer policy ownership.","$400 premium of $500 budget leaves $100 monthly for home cash; in 48 months this is $4,800 before interest, no guaranteed $15k home down payment."]}},{"id":"L13.S3","title":"Deeds, Beneficiaries and Probate","subtitle":"Connecticut property title, funded trusts, incapacity, probate workflow, mortgages and heir coordination","prerequisites":["L1.S1 ownership and liabilities","L6.S2 mortgage deed, equity and lending","L12.S2 inherited retirement beneficiary obligations","L13.S1 beneficiary protection needs"],"objectives":["Build an asset-by-asset transfer map including jointly held assets and beneficiary contracts","Distinguish survivorship, tenancy in common, probate title and trust ownership","Identify probate duties and Connecticut estate-tax return filing even below tax threshold","Explain the difference between signing a trust and legally funding it","Analyze mortgage due-on-sale statutory protections and risks during trust transfers","Quantify the estate's actual net transferable home equity after debt and costs","Create an incapacity and after-death implementation checklist"],"sections":[{"title":"Estate transfer is an asset-by-asset legal system","body":["On death, a family does not simply hand its net worth statement to the heirs. Real property follows the deed and applicable succession law; bank and brokerage accounts may pass by payable-on-death/transfer-on-death designation, survivorship or estate administration; life insurance follows the contract's beneficiary; retirement accounts follow beneficiary and federal rules. The will controls assets subject to the decedent's probate estate but generally cannot override a valid separate beneficiary designation.","Create a ledger for each item: legal owner, current market value, associated debt, title form, named beneficiary, contingent beneficiary, probate status, applicable taxes and responsible administrator. For a $300,000 home with $200,000 mortgage, the economic equity starts at $100,000, but that is before liens, repairs, sale expenses and legal costs. Net worth is not the same as cash immediately available to children.","Separate ownership from intended inheritance. A married person might want the spouse to occupy a home and the child eventually to inherit; an outright deed transfer, a will and a trust can produce different results depending on marital rights and beneficiary language. The correct starting point is the actual recorded deed and signed designations, not the owner's recollection of a planning conversation."],"worked":["Home $300,000; mortgage $200,000; simplified equity $100,000.","Assume 6% sale costs on $300,000 => $18,000, reducing sale proceeds net of mortgage to $82,000 before other estate expenses.","A trust naming an heir without an actual deed transfer is not proof that trust owns the home.","List owner, debt, beneficiary and creditor claims separately to avoid overstating accessible inheritance."],"practice":[["Does a will necessarily override a valid life-insurance beneficiary designation?","Generally no. The insurance contract designation can control outside the will, subject to relevant legal constraints."],["Can an heir assume a $300,000 mortgage-encumbered house is equivalent to $300,000 of liquid inheritance?","No. Debt, sale costs, liens and timing affect net realizable value."]]},{"title":"Deeds and tenancy: survivorship is not a universal default","body":["Real property can be held in various forms: individual title, tenancy in common, joint tenancy with right of survivorship where legally established, and trust-owned title. Exact available forms and consequences depend on state law and deed wording. Tenancy in common generally allows a deceased owner's fractional interest to pass under an estate or other governing arrangement, while valid survivorship ownership can transfer the surviving owner's interest outside ordinary probate title distribution.","An individual cannot safely create survivorship merely by adding a family member's name without reviewing mortgage terms, gift consequences and creditor risk. Co-owners may have rights to partition or attach interests, and a child's divorce or judgments can complicate a parent's housing security. For Connecticut real property, a recorded deed and competent local examination establish what actually changed.","Suppose an unmarried owner with 100% title wishes one child to inherit. A funded revocable trust could hold title during life, subject to mortgage and drafting review, while a will could route probate assets via a pour-over provision. Neither automatically eliminates the need to handle claims, taxes or deeds properly. Probate avoidance is only one objective among control, cost, tax basis, creditor and incapacity issues."],"worked":["Hypothetical house value $300,000, owner A has 50% tenancy-in-common share with B.","A's gross economic share is $150,000 before share-specific liens and expenses.","It does not automatically become B's property merely because B co-owns the other half.","A valid right-of-survivorship arrangement would produce different title outcomes, subject to applicable state law."],"practice":[["Does ordinary tenancy in common always pass a deceased co-owner's share to surviving co-owner?","No. That differs from survivorship title; the deceased person's share may pass through the estate or other valid transfer structure."],["Why should a homeowner avoid adding a child to a deed solely for probate avoidance?","It can create gift, creditor, tax-basis, divorce, control and financing risks that outweigh expected probate benefits."]]},{"title":"What Connecticut probate courts actually do","body":["Connecticut Probate Courts administer deceased persons' estates, adjudicate some will and trust disputes, establish title to certain assets and oversee fiduciaries in defined cases. Estate administration can require an appointed fiduciary, identifying property, notifying creditors and beneficiaries, paying valid claims and taxes, and distributing what remains. Probate is not by definition a 'government confiscation' of family property; it is a legal process designed partly to settle competing rights.","A revocable living trust may reduce formal probate requirements for assets actually funded into the trust, but assets left individually titled can still need probate proceedings. A pour-over will provides a backstop for some property but generally does not erase the need for probate administration of unfunded assets. Estate and probate tax reporting can remain even for nonprobate assets.","Under current 2026 Connecticut guidance, estates at or below $15 million of Connecticut taxable estate generally file a CT-706 NT with the appropriate Probate Court, subject to specific exceptions such as a state QTIP election, even if no Connecticut estate tax is due. Over the threshold, CT-706/709 filing with the Department of Revenue Services and Probate Court is generally required. Distinguish no tax liability from no tax or court filing responsibilities."],"worked":["Connecticut resident dies in 2026 with stipulated $500,000 Connecticut taxable estate.","This is below the $15 million estate tax exemption so Connecticut estate tax is generally $0 under simplified assumptions.","However, a CT-706 NT Probate Court filing may still be required.","Probate fees and legal expenses are not the same as state estate tax and can exist even when tax is zero."],"practice":[["If Connecticut estate tax is $0, does that always eliminate state estate return filing?","No. CT-706 NT can still be required with the Probate Court, with exceptions and rules described by DRS."],["Does creating a revocable trust remove all creditors and probate court obligations?","No. Funding, asset ownership, applicable creditor rights, court processes and tax filings must still be evaluated."]]},{"title":"Funding a revocable trust is an administrative act","body":["A revocable living trust is a legal relationship governed by its signed terms and trustee powers. Creating a document does not automatically assign a house, brokerage account or bank balance to the trust. Real property typically requires appropriate deeds and land-record steps; certain account ownership changes require the custodian's paperwork. Beneficiary designations may be coordinated with a trust but should not be changed blindly, especially for tax-sensitive retirement accounts.","Consider a household signing a trust that directs a daughter to inherit the house. If the deed remains solely in the parent's name and no effective transfer or other mechanism operates, the trust may not directly own or control that house at death. The transfer ledger should list proof: deed recording book/page, bank trust certificate, trustee identification and confirmed beneficiary forms. An unfunded document is not proof of completed planning.","The trustee must be capable of administering property, filing tax returns, keeping accounts and following distribution standards after death or incapacity. A trustee appointment without permission to access relevant records, maintain insurance and pay mortgage expenses can cause avoidable delays. Review funding after refinancing, moving, changing jobs and opening new accounts."],"worked":["Trust instrument signed; deed remains in owner's sole individual name.","Trust-controlled real estate assets evidenced by recorded transfer: $0, unless another operative arrangement applies.","A $300,000 house is still $300,000 of owned property but not necessarily an asset owned by that trust.","After properly executed and recorded deed transfer (assuming valid), trust property ledger can include the house subject to mortgage."],"practice":[["Does listing a house on a schedule attached to a trust always replace a properly executed real-estate deed?","No. Title law and transfer formalities govern; a recorded deed or other valid conveyance may be necessary."],["What should a post-funding audit verify?","Official deed/custodian ownership records, insurer and mortgage documents, trustee contact and updated beneficiary designations."]]},{"title":"Mortgage due-on-sale clauses and transfer to living trusts","body":["Moving a mortgaged home into a living trust raises a question under loan contracts. The federal Garn–St. Germain Depository Institutions Act restricts a lender's ability to enforce a due-on-sale clause for certain transfers into an inter vivos trust where the borrower is and remains a beneficiary and the transfer does not relate to transfer of occupancy rights. That statutory protection is specific; it does not guarantee every transaction involving any trust is free from lender objections or administrative requirements.","The statute does not itself forgive the mortgage or make a child immune to lender claims. The note, mortgage security instrument, homeowners insurance, title insurer and the terms of down-payment assistance or state housing programs can create separate conditions. A homeowner should obtain a local property/estate attorney's review and follow necessary lender and insurer notification requirements rather than assuming a copy-pasted deed will preserve every benefit.","If a home is worth $300,000 and debt $220,000, transferring the deed to a qualifying revocable trust generally does not create $80,000 additional equity. The economic value and debt remain in place; only title/control and later transition procedures may change. A deed transfer may also have different implications for Medicaid eligibility, homestead protection, state/local taxes or refinance underwriting."],"worked":["Home $300,000; mortgage $220,000; equity $80,000 before costs.","Deed transfer to a qualifying revocable living trust does not extinguish $220,000 note or create new net worth.","Statutory due-on-sale exception is conditional on trust/borrower/occupancy facts.","A transfer to an ILIT or an unrelated family trust must not be assumed to qualify for the same statutory exception."],"practice":[["Does every transfer of mortgaged property to any trust automatically qualify for federal due-on-sale protection?","No. The statutory inter vivos trust exception includes beneficiary and occupancy conditions and does not override every other legal/contractual issue."],["Does placing the mortgaged home in a trust pay off the mortgage?","No. The lien and loan obligations remain unless properly discharged."]]},{"title":"Incapacity planning and fiduciary continuity","body":["Estate planning covers the years when an owner is alive but cannot manage accounts. A durable financial power of attorney, healthcare representative, advance directive, trustee succession provisions and an organized account inventory solve different problems. A revocable trust can give a successor trustee authority over trust assets, while assets left outside the trust may require a properly executed power of attorney or a court-appointed conservator.","An incapacitated homeowner may still owe mortgage, property tax, utilities and insurance. Family members need legitimate authority to pay those obligations and maintain asset records. Without proper designation, a spouse or adult child may not have access to accounts despite a good relationship with the owner. Powers of attorney and trustee authority can be limited or subject to acceptance rules; documents need local review.","Modern household planning also includes securely organized digital account access, the location of originals, business revenue, recurring bills and tax documents. The goal is to let fiduciaries respond promptly without sharing private passwords indiscriminately or violating account-provider policies. Include contact lists for insurance carriers, mortgage servicer, accountant and qualified attorney."],"worked":["Monthly housing obligations $1,200 rent/mortgage equivalent + $300 tax/insurance + $200 utilities = $1,700.","A three-month incapacitation period creates $5,100 housing cash needs before other living costs.","Trustee/agent authority may permit proper management but does not create the needed $5,100 in cash.","Hold an accessible emergency reserve with clear authorized access rules."],"practice":[["Why is a will alone not an incapacity plan?","A will primarily governs after death; during life, agents, healthcare decision makers, and successor trustees need separate legal authority."],["Why must trust funding be reconciled with powers of attorney?","A trustee may control only trust-held property; assets outside the trust may need an agent or other authority."]]},{"title":"Beneficiary designations, retirement accounts and children","body":["Life insurance, workplace plans and IRAs often transfer under beneficiary designations rather than through will provisions. Verify primary and contingent beneficiaries and the specific name/relationship details each custodian has recorded. An old beneficiary form can unintentionally direct assets away from current dependents. Marriage and qualified retirement plan spousal-consent rights can limit simple attempts to redirect certain benefits.","Naming a minor child outright can require guardianship or restricted administration rather than immediate independent control of money. A carefully drafted trust can specify ages, support standards, disability benefits protection and trustee authority. But naming a trust as retirement account beneficiary can change required-minimum-distribution classification and tax treatment; the beneficiary plan should be built with retirement tax law, not simply copied from life insurance.","Benefits paid to disabled dependents may affect means-tested public programs. A special needs trust or ABLE account can have roles, but their rules and limits need specialist review. The family should distinguish supporting a child from creating a tax-only mechanism; practical continuity, caregivers and trustees matter as much as the legal title."],"worked":["Retirement account $120,000 and life insurance $300,000, both intended to support a daughter.","Combined nominal intended support $420,000 before possible taxes, account distribution restrictions and costs.","One outdated beneficiary form for $120,000 can reroute approximately 28.6% of the nominal intended assets.","A checklist catches this risk without requiring any new financial product purchase."],"practice":[["Why may naming an individual minor child directly create additional administration?","Minors generally lack full legal capacity to manage large assets and may need guardianship or trust management depending on jurisdiction."],["Does naming a trust as IRA beneficiary guarantee all inherited IRAs avoid income tax?","No. Beneficiary classifications and required distributions affect taxes; drafting must coordinate with retirement law."]]},{"title":"The Connecticut deed and probate implementation file","body":["A complete estate implementation packet contains current recorded deeds, a title analysis, mortgage statements, property insurance, documented bank/brokerage account titles, every beneficiary form, wills, trust documents, agent appointments and an administration timeline. The analyst must distinguish intended changes from changes already legally effective. A 'trust-owned' label in a spreadsheet does not transfer real property.","Compare three approaches for a hypothetical $300,000 Connecticut home with $200,000 mortgage: individual title plus will, valid survivorship title where appropriate, and funded revocable trust. Each has probate, tax basis, control, creditor and administrative differences; no strategy erases the $200,000 debt. Evaluate whether heirs will keep the home, sell it or refinance and the availability of cash to pay property taxes while title is resolved.","The final memo identifies an estate attorney's decision points and operational steps—not just a generic recommendation to 'put the home in a trust.' These include current deed form, marital property rights, lender and insurance conditions, gift-tax impact of deeds, local land record fees, trust terms, property tax benefits and future down-payment assistance eligibility."],"worked":["Home value $300,000 and mortgage $200,000 imply $100,000 gross equity.","At 6% hypothetical selling costs, $18,000 costs leave $82,000 before other expenses.","Probate-avoidance instrument changes legal process but not these basic economic figures.","Document surviving occupant's cash needs; ownership transfer without liquidity may be ineffective."],"practice":[["What is the difference between a trust signed and a funded trust?","A signed document establishes terms; funding requires valid transfers or designations giving the trust enforceable ownership/control over applicable assets."],["What is an estate attorney's first practical input for a deed plan?","The current recorded deed, mortgage documents, occupancy and family beneficiary objectives, rather than an assumed universal deed form."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Connecticut Probate Courts trusts and estate administration","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates"],["Connecticut DRS 2026 estate/gift filing thresholds","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/individual-income-tax-portal/estate-and-gift-taxes/tax-information"],["US Code 12 USC 1701j-3 due on sale transfers to trusts","https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-118/t12/s1701j-3/"],["Connecticut probate fee and expense calculator","https://www.ctprobate.gov/fees-expenses-calculators"],["IRS Publication 559 estates, executors and beneficiaries","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p559"],["IRS inherited IRA beneficiary rules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-beneficiary"],["IRS federal estate tax inclusion and filing","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i706"],["Connecticut Department of Consumer Protection real estate resources","https://portal.ct.gov/dcp"]],"assessment":{"title":"Connecticut family asset-transfer and incapacity audit","prompt":"Create the complete ownership/transfer matrix for a fictional $300,000 Connecticut home with $200,000 mortgage, two bank accounts, a $120,000 IRA, a $300,000 life policy, vehicle, and one jointly owned account. Compare current individual deed plus will versus survivorship and funded revocable trust with a daughter as intended beneficiary. Calculate home net proceeds assuming 6% sale cost; explain CT-706 NT, due-on-sale exception conditions, mortgage obligations, incapacity agents, IRA trust beneficiaries and minor-child risks. Build a six-month implementation roadmap with attorney review gates.","rubric":["Deeds and estate versus nonprobate distinctions (25%)","Connecticut filing and probate process (20%)","Trust funding, successor authority and mortgage review (20%)","Beneficiary/retirement/minor planning (20%)","Cash-flow, net equity and evidence quality (15%)"],"answerKey":["House $300k−mortgage $200k = $100k pre-sale equity; 6% sale expense $18k leads $82k gross net sale liquidity before other costs.","Trust is not automatically funded by a signed instrument; proper title/deed/documentary proof is required.","CT-706 NT may be required despite $0 CT estate tax for qualifying under-$15m 2026 estate.","Garn–St. Germain due-on-sale exception for certain inter vivos trusts requires borrower remaining beneficiary and occupancy-right condition; not every trust transfer qualifies.","IRA beneficiary and life-insurance ownership require separate custodian/insurer records and tax planning.","Incapacity plan adds durable attorney, healthcare documents, trustee succession and secured fiduciary access."]}},{"id":"L13.S4","title":"Estate Tax, Basis & Trust Types","subtitle":"Federal and Connecticut transfer taxation, stepped basis, gifts, irrevocable trusts, estate liquidity and generational stewardship","prerequisites":["L11.S3 capital gain and basis","L12.S2 inherited retirement-account tax rules","L13.S2 policy ownership and insurance proceeds","L13.S3 funded trusts and property succession"],"objectives":["Distinguish estate tax, gift tax, income tax, inheritance and probate costs","Use accurate federal and Connecticut 2026 $15m basic estate exclusion thresholds with scope limits","Compute gift annual exclusions and understand Form 709 and lifetime exclusion concepts","Distinguish gift carryover basis from qualifying date-of-death basis adjustments","Compare revocable grantor trust and irrevocable non-grantor trusts on ownership and control","Explain why estate exclusion, basis benefits and beneficiary income tax may conflict","Build a defensible family estate strategy for housing, adult children and retirement accounts"],"sections":[{"title":"Estate tax, income tax, gift tax and probate fees are four different calculations","body":["A decedent's estate can owe tax on qualifying large asset transfers at death; the decedent, estate, trust or beneficiaries can owe different income tax on earnings, sales or distributions; lifetime donors can encounter gift-tax reporting or tax; and court administration may produce filing and probate fees. These are independent systems. The phrase 'tax-free inheritance' can hide the difference between receiving an inherited principal amount without income inclusion and later recognizing gain when selling an inherited asset.","The gross estate for transfer-tax purposes can include home, savings, retirement accounts, business interests and certain life insurance proceeds under ownership and inclusion rules. Estate liabilities and deductions affect taxable transfer value. Probate assets do not equal federal gross estate; a policy paid directly to a named beneficiary can still be included in the insured's estate under incidents-of-ownership rules.","For an illustrative $500,000 household, a 2026 federal or Connecticut estate tax is generally unlikely under basic assumptions, yet income tax on inherited pretax IRA withdrawals, property tax, probate costs, liens and administration can still matter. Planning should prioritize maintaining housing, identifying beneficiaries, reducing family disputes and documenting succession authority."],"worked":["Home $300,000 + IRA $150,000 + brokerage $40,000 + bank $10,000 = $500,000 asset value.","Net equity and estate deductions vary independently; $500,000 assets are well below 2026 $15m basic transfer-tax exclusions.","Federal income tax may still apply to inherited pretax IRA withdrawals.","Court filing and legal fees cannot be set to zero merely because estate tax is not due."],"practice":[["If a decedent leaves a $100,000 pretax IRA, does the federal estate-tax exclusion necessarily make heirs' withdrawals income-tax-free?","No. IRA inherited distributions can create ordinary taxable income even where no estate tax is due."],["Can an asset outside probate still be included in the federal gross estate?","Yes. Certain nonprobate assets, including life insurance under particular ownership circumstances, can enter federal estate inclusion."]]},{"title":"The actual 2026 exclusion: $15 million federal and Connecticut","body":["The IRS Form 706 instructions specify a $15,000,000 basic exclusion for decedents dying during 2026. The federal transfer tax uses a unified framework for certain lifetime taxable gifts and estate transfers; merely having a taxable estate above the threshold does not imply multiplying the full estate by 40%. The basic exclusion, deductions, credits, previous gifts, portability election and transfer-tax base must be reconstructed.","Connecticut's Department of Revenue Services independently states a $15,000,000 estate-tax exemption for 2026 and related gift-tax exemption framework. Qualified Connecticut taxable estates above the threshold generally face a 12% Connecticut estate-tax rate on the specified taxable excess under current guidance, with additional rules. A Connecticut estate below the exemption generally still needs CT-706 NT with Probate Court in relevant cases, not a federal return automatically.","A married couple should not automatically double exclusions in a spreadsheet. Portability of a deceased spouse's unused federal exclusion generally requires a timely and valid federal Form 706 election, with exceptions and extensions in certain circumstances; Connecticut planning can differ and has QTIP election rules. A report should label whose exclusion is used, past taxable gifts, estate inclusion, residence, valuation and filing."],"worked":["2026 federal basic exclusion for an individual: $15,000,000.","2026 Connecticut estate-tax exemption for a qualifying resident: $15,000,000.","Simple Connecticut taxable estate $15,400,000 and no special credits/previous gifts could suggest 12%×$400,000=$48,000 under simplified state formula.","The calculation is illustrative only; federal transfer-tax reconciliation and CT rules need tax return instructions."],"practice":[["Does the federal basic exclusion automatically shield each spouse's full $15 million without filing or estate planning?","No. Applicable inclusion, lifetime gifts, portability election and spouse-specific facts determine usable exclusion."],["If the estate has no Connecticut estate tax, can one assume no Connecticut Probate Court tax form is needed?","No. CT-706 NT requirements may still apply to estates at or below threshold."]]},{"title":"Annual gifting is a reporting threshold, not a magic wealth multiplier","body":["For 2026, the federal gift-tax annual exclusion is $19,000 per donee from one donor for qualifying present-interest gifts, or potentially $38,000 from two spouses making qualifying gifts under applicable rules. The exclusion generally determines how much can be transferred to a donee annually without being treated as a taxable gift for gift-tax reporting purposes; it is not an annual income-tax deduction to the donor.","Amounts exceeding the exclusion can require Form 709 reporting and ordinarily use lifetime gift-and-estate exclusion before actual federal gift tax is payable; the existence of a filing requirement does not automatically mean the giver must write a tax check. Trust contributions are not necessarily present-interest gifts entitled to the annual exclusion without proper beneficiary rights. Connecticut has its own gift return and aggregate taxable gift framework.","Example: one parent gives $29,000 cash outright to one child in 2026 and no special exclusions apply. The first $19,000 can fall under the annual exclusion, leaving $10,000 as a reportable taxable gift that could use remaining lifetime exclusion without immediate gift tax. The parent has not reduced income tax by $29,000. Gifts to minors, educational payments and medical payments can involve special direct-payment exceptions or legal structures."],"worked":["Qualifying present-interest cash gift $29,000; 2026 federal annual exclusion $19,000.","Illustrative amount above annual exclusion $10,000, generally reportable on Form 709 under qualifying facts.","Assuming sufficient unused lifetime exclusion and no unusual facts, actual gift tax owed may be $0.","Donor does not deduct the $29,000 ordinary personal gift from federal taxable income."],"practice":[["Does a 2026 gift of $29,000 to an adult child necessarily generate $10,000 cash tax owed?","No. The excess over the annual exclusion can be a taxable/reportable gift using lifetime exemption, with no immediate gift tax if sufficient exclusion remains."],["Is a contribution to any trust automatically eligible for the $19,000 present-interest annual exclusion?","No. The trust beneficiary's present-interest rights and other legal requirements must be verified."]]},{"title":"Inherited basis and lifetime gift basis are economically distinct","body":["For many capital assets included in a decedent's estate, Internal Revenue Code §1014 generally adjusts income-tax basis to fair market value at death or an authorized alternative valuation basis, subject to exceptions. A lifetime gift generally gives the recipient carryover basis for gain, with special dual-basis and other adjustments possible. One must not use the familiar phrase 'step-up' as a guarantee for every asset, especially retirement accounts or certain irrevocable trust arrangements.","Assume a house or taxable stock portfolio had $150,000 adjusted tax basis and is worth $300,000 at death. If a qualifying inherited asset receives basis of $300,000, and the heir later sells it for $310,000 before selling expenses, illustrative capital gain is $10,000. If the same asset was gifted during life with $150,000 carryover basis for gain, a $310,000 later sale can produce $160,000 gain before adjustments. The actual tax rate and exclusions depend on asset type and individual facts.","Income in respect of a decedent such as many traditional retirement withdrawals does not generally receive the same appreciated-capital-asset basis reset. Property held in a completed-gift irrevocable grantor trust excluded from the grantor's gross estate generally does not receive §1014 adjustment solely because the grantor was treated as owner for income tax; IRS Revenue Ruling 2023-02 addresses this specific trap."],"worked":["Original adjusted basis $150,000; value at death $300,000; later sale $310,000.","Qualifying inherited §1014 basis $300,000 ⇒ gain $10,000 before selling costs.","Hypothetical lifetime gift carryover basis $150,000 ⇒ later gain $160,000 before adjustments.","Difference in recognized gain $150,000; tax savings depend on rates and eligibility, not the gain difference alone."],"practice":[["Does every asset in an irrevocable grantor trust receive a step-up merely because the grantor dies?","No. IRS Revenue Ruling 2023-02 addresses non-included irrevocable grantor-trust assets that generally do not qualify for §1014 basis adjustment."],["Can an inherited traditional IRA receive the same automatic market-value basis reset as inherited appreciated stock?","Generally no. Pretax retirement distributions commonly remain income in respect of a decedent and have different income-tax treatment."]]},{"title":"Revocable trust: management and probate flexibility, not automatic estate tax relief","body":["A revocable living trust generally allows the grantor to amend or revoke the arrangement while alive and competent, subject to its terms. The trust can help coordinate management and successor control of properly funded property, but assets remain economically attributable to the grantor for many tax and creditor purposes. Trust property may remain included in the taxable gross estate; creation alone does not convert a $500,000 family's wealth into a tax-free shelter.","A funded revocable trust can direct post-death distributions for an adult child, future education or housing maintenance, but it still requires an informed successor trustee, recordkeeping and clear distribution powers. Without coordinated deeds, beneficiary designations and retirement account treatment, a well-drafted document might be largely unfunded. Trustee fees, attorney drafting costs and administration time must be weighed against probate and control benefits.","The home remains subject to property tax, mortgage and insurance obligations. Some mortgage transfers into certain living trusts have federal due-on-sale protection conditioned on borrower-beneficiary and occupancy facts; this does not immunize the deed from assistance program requirements, creditors or tax treatment. A proper recommendation coordinates local legal title, estate liquidity and intended living arrangements."],"worked":["Revocable trust holds stipulated $300,000 house and $200,000 mortgage.","Economic equity remains $100,000 before sale costs; establishing trust does not create additional $100,000.","If grantor can revoke and retains control, family should not claim automatic removal from taxable gross estate.","Practical benefit may be smoother successor administration if deed/title and trustee documents are valid."],"practice":[["Does signing a revocable trust ordinarily remove all its assets from the grantor's taxable estate?","No. Retained control and applicable inclusion rules typically keep such property within the grantor's estate."],["What is the main operational weakness of a trust document that has not been funded?","Property and accounts not validly transferred may remain outside direct trustee authority and still require other administration."]]},{"title":"Irrevocable trusts trade personal control for defined beneficiary benefits","body":["Irrevocable trust structures differ widely. An intentionally structured life insurance trust may own insurance and limit insured's incidents of ownership; a special needs trust may protect benefit eligibility; a spendthrift trust can control distribution timing; and certain charitable or marital trusts can accomplish distinct objectives. No trust form universally removes taxes or defeats creditors. Each structure must identify settlor, property, trustee, beneficiaries, distribution standard and legal purpose.","Transferring appreciated assets to some irrevocable trusts can reduce the grantor's future estate inclusion but may also sacrifice future inherited-basis adjustment, while exposing the trust's income to compressed tax brackets or specialized grantor rules. Paying tax currently as grantor may be intentional in one structure and irrelevant in another. The trust may file Form 1041 and provide Schedules K-1 depending on its income-tax character and distributions.","An irrevocable trust is not a private bank to borrow from without consequence. Trustee loans to beneficiaries, distributions to family, security interests and self-dealing can violate fiduciary duties or alter intended tax treatment. A household seeking a down payment should not contribute its only savings to a structure that requires relinquishing control without specialist advice."],"worked":["Settlor transfers $100,000 appreciating property into an irrevocable trust.","Economic family value remains $100,000 before transaction costs; it has not magically doubled.","Grantor may lose unilateral access; beneficiary receives rights defined by document, not an automatic checking account.","Estate exclusion and loss of basis adjustment can work in opposing directions, requiring after-tax scenario comparison."],"practice":[["Is an irrevocable trust inherently a tax-free account with unrestricted homeowner withdrawals?","No. Trustee powers, beneficiary rights, grantor status, income taxes and fiduciary duties determine access and outcomes."],["Why might transferring appreciated property out of an estate reduce one tax but increase another?","Estate transfer-tax benefits may be offset by carryover basis and larger future capital gains when property is later sold."]]},{"title":"Insurance, estate inclusion and the three-year transfer problem","body":["Life insurance death proceeds generally are not included in a beneficiary's federal gross income when paid because of an insured person's death under qualifying facts. That income-tax exclusion is not the same question as whether policy proceeds are includible in the insured's gross estate for transfer-tax purposes. If the insured held incidents of ownership, policy death proceeds may be included even when paid directly to another named beneficiary.","An irrevocable life insurance trust can own a policy and govern benefit use, but successful estate-tax exclusion depends on details including ownership structure, insured control and transfer rules. A policy transferred out of the insured's ownership within three years before death may be pulled back into the estate under IRC §2035 in relevant circumstances. A newly issued policy initially acquired by an appropriately designed trust can produce different legal treatment than reassigning an existing policy.","Even in a correct ILIT structure, trust assets cannot necessarily be used at the insured's whim for a home purchase; trustees owe duties to beneficiaries. Paying premiums can be treated as gifts to the trust with their own annual exclusion and notice design. For a family far below a $15m estate-tax threshold, the complexity and lost control may outweigh speculative transfer-tax benefits."],"worked":["Policy death benefit $500,000, insured personally owns contractual incidents of ownership.","Beneficiary can generally exclude qualifying death proceeds from income; estate inclusion may still be $500,000 under ownership rule.","Owner transfers policy to ILIT and dies 18 months later: potential IRC §2035 three-year inclusion issue.","Moving policy name into a trust does not erase cost, insurer charges, existing loans or ownership lookback risks."],"practice":[["Do tax-free beneficiary death proceeds prove that death benefit was excluded from estate valuation?","No. Income-tax exclusion for recipient and estate inclusion of policy proceeds are separate rules."],["Can a person assume transferring an existing policy to an ILIT removes it from estate immediately regardless of death timing?","No. The three-year inclusion rule may apply to certain transfers."]]},{"title":"Funding for an heir: education, home maintenance and long-run governance","body":["A practical generational plan should answer what an heir actually needs, when funds are required and who will administer them. A house passed without a budget for taxes, utilities, insurance or repairs may leave heirs asset-rich but unable to retain the property. A trustee can be directed to fund education, reasonable housing expenses or long-term care under valid terms, but the distribution standard must be clear and administrable.","Suppose a property carries $4,800 annual property tax and insurance, $3,000 maintenance reserve and $1,800 utilities. Retaining the property costs $9,600 each year before mortgage payments and special assessments. If liquid estate assets are only $8,000, the house may require immediate outside income. An estate liquidity policy might direct use of existing liquid funds or qualifying insurance proceeds, subject to ownership and beneficiary structure, rather than rely on rushed asset sales.","A family wealth plan also includes successor trustees, independent fiduciary oversight, beneficiary contact information, updated asset inventories, dispute resolution and document storage. A trust can help govern funds but never replaces education about spending, property ownership and tax filing. Test the plan with a temporary disability and a survivor facing two years of reduced income, not only with an assumed death at old age."],"worked":["Property annual costs $4,800 tax/insurance + $3,000 repairs + $1,800 utilities = $9,600.","Two-year reserve = $19,200 before mortgage and extraordinary costs.","Existing liquid estate reserve $8,000 leaves a $11,200 two-year shortfall.","A funded trust with no liquid assets cannot manufacture the required $11,200 cash."],"practice":[["Why is inheritance of a valuable home not enough to guarantee heirs can keep it?","Mortgage, taxes, insurance, maintenance and repairs require continuing cash flow regardless of asset value."],["What is a practical benefit of a clear successor trustee and asset inventory?","They allow lawful, timely payment of bills and administration without searching for ownership records during a crisis."]]},{"title":"An integrated Connecticut estate strategy without magical tax promises","body":["The final planning sequence identifies goals, owners and dependents, then quantifies the estate's economic value, contract rights, titles and current cash needs. It separately models federal estate tax, Connecticut estate tax, income tax basis, retirement-income tax and administrative fees. In 2026, most modest estates will not owe a transfer tax, but beneficiary errors, unrecorded deed transfers, taxable retirement withdrawals or policy lapse can still inflict substantial losses.","Choose between a will with appropriate beneficiary designations, a funded revocable trust, and specialized irrevocable structures only after comparing actual needs and legal consequences. An ILIT may be useful when a significant estate-tax exposure exists, but its cost and restrictions are not a shortcut to borrowing policy cash value freely. For an intended child heir, preserve housing stability and long-term stewardship while respecting rights of spouse and other beneficiaries.","A rigorous report names unanswered facts, including legal marital status, owner/title, outstanding liens, future residency, plan beneficiaries, policy MEC status, basis records and desired inheritance timing. State the accountant and estate attorney's assignments. The financial analyst should not claim to execute a legal trust, erase debt, guarantee probate avoidance or make a tax liability disappear."],"worked":["Hypothetical Connecticut home $300k, loan $200k, taxable stock $40k, pretax IRA $60k, liquid cash $10k.","Gross listed assets $410k before insurance death proceeds, with loan $200k => $210k net worth, subject to other liabilities.","2026 federal and CT basic estate exemptions each $15m under ordinary simplified assumptions; estate tax likely $0.","Estate still needs documented beneficiaries, debt/maintenance funding, basis record and Connecticut tax/probate filing review."],"practice":[["What should dominate planning for a modest estate far below $15 million?","Continuity, beneficiaries, legal title, emergency liquidity, healthcare, inherited retirement taxes, property upkeep and affordable administration."],["Is it sufficient to conclude 'put everything into an irrevocable trust' based on a broad tax-savings claim?","No. Trust type, loss of control, gifting, basis, income tax, administrative expense, beneficiary needs and legal requirements must be separately analyzed."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS July 2026 Form 706 estate tax instructions $15m exclusion","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i706"],["Connecticut DRS 2026 estate and gift tax guidance","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/individual-income-tax-portal/estate-and-gift-taxes/tax-information"],["IRS 2026 annual gift exclusion of $19k","https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/frequently-asked-questions-on-gift-taxes"],["IRS basis treatment under Revenue Ruling 2023-02","https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rr-23-02.pdf"],["IRS Publication 559 for heirs, executors and administrators","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p559"],["IRS Form 709 gift tax instructions","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i709"],["IRS insurance beneficiary gross income","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"],["CT Probate Courts trust and estate administration","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates"],["US Code 26 USC 1014 basis of property acquired from a decedent","https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title26-section1014"],["IRS reporting of trust and estate income Form 1041","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1041"]],"assessment":{"title":"Federal/Connecticut estate-tax, income basis and lifetime gift comparative memorandum","prompt":"Calculate 2026 federal and Connecticut basic exclusions and assess tax filing for a fictional $500,000 estate. Compare a $150,000-basis capital asset worth $300,000 at death and sold by heir for $310,000 under (A) qualifying inherited §1014 basis and (B) $150k carryover-basis lifetime gift. Analyze one person's $29k present-interest cash gift to an adult child versus 2026 $19k annual exclusion. Model the ownership/estate inclusion of a personally owned $500k life policy, a policy transferred 18 months before death to ILIT, and a newly issued trust-owned policy. Analyze a $300k CT house with $200k mortgage and $9,600 annual nonmortgage costs over two years against $8k cash. Produce a three-option estate plan with fiduciary, reporting, tax and control tradeoffs.","rubric":["Federal and CT 2026 thresholds and tax versus reporting distinction (20%)","Gift and basis calculations including trust basis hazards (25%)","Revocable/irrevocable trust and life estate inclusion analysis (25%)","Liquidity, mortgage and beneficiary support plan (20%)","Primary sources, uncertainty and legal review limits (10%)"],"answerKey":["2026 federal basic exclusion $15m and Connecticut estate-tax exemption $15m; a $500k ordinary taxable estate has $0 transfer tax under simplifying assumptions, yet CT-706 NT may be required.","Qualifying acquired-from-decedent basis $300k yields $10k sale gain; $150k carryover gift basis can yield $160k gain, before adjustments.","$29k qualifying present-interest gift less $19k annual exclusion leaves $10k generally reportable taxable gift, usually no immediate tax if lifetime exclusion sufficient.","Personally owned insured's policy can be in estate despite beneficiary death proceeds income exclusion. Transferred existing policy may face IRC §2035 three-year rule.","Two-year property carry $9,600×2=$19,200, less $8k liquid reserve = $11,200 shortfall.","ILIT may cause loss of personal owner control; do not assume insured can borrow trust policy as free personal collateral."]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Module 13 — Connecticut family protection and intergenerational estate committee","intro":"Four related hypothetical analyses, with documented and independent assumptions. Distinguish death insurance risk, accessible funds, legal title, estate-tax inclusion, creditor claims, survivor support and basis. Current 2026 laws cited below; all market returns, pricing and client facts are teaching hypotheticals, not product quotes or legal advice.","scenario":["Household: Connecticut homeowner and spouse intend that a daughter eventually have housing security; mortgage $220,000, education needs $30,000, final costs $10,000, 20 years of household support expected. Available unrestricted liquid savings $40,000; verify if that cash is also held for an upcoming closing or emergency fund.","Human capital: $30,000 annual REAL household contribution for 20 years; hypothetical 3% REAL discount rate. The needs worksheet uses a separately stipulated $250,000 survivor support allocation, which must not be mechanically added to present value of the same support without reconciliation.","Protection: permanent policy premium hypothetical $250/month versus comparable term $35/month; invest only the $215 difference at illustrated 6% nominal monthly-compounded return for 20 years, excluding taxes/fees.","Disability: $2,700 monthly essentials; 90-day wait period; hypothetical monthly disability benefit $1,900 after waiting; existing separate emergency fund $6,000.","Policy loans: hypothetical $15,000 initial loan at 7% capitalized annually for five years; second model $30,000 at 8% for five years and only $42,000 stressed surrender value.","Tax: insurer report gives $40,000 investment in contract, $60,000 gross surrender value and $35,000 loan satisfied upon surrender. An assumed stable 22% federal income tax rate is for illustrative incremental modeling only.","Deeds: house worth $300,000 with $200,000 lien in a second estate scenario; sale expense assumed 6% of sale price; no other liens and no basis information for sale-tax calculation.","Estate transfer: plan compares individual deed plus will, properly funded revocable trust, survivorship with spouse and specialist irrevocable trust only if factually justified.","Assets: an IRA $120,000, life policy $300,000 and other accounts; daughter may be a beneficiary and might inherit only after both spouses have died. Retirement beneficiary income tax is separate.","Estate-tax rules: 2026 federal and CT exclusion $15m, Connecticut CT-706 NT filing may still apply if no estate tax due, federal annual gift exclusion $19k per qualifying donee.","Basis: $150,000 adjusted basis in qualifying property appreciating to $300,000 at date of death; assumed sale $310,000; compare valid §1014 inheritance to lifetime gift with gain basis carried over.","Inheritance liquidity: property tax/insurance $4,800 yearly, ongoing repairs $3,000, utilities $1,800; estate liquid reserve only $8,000. Two-year property carrying cost before mortgage and special assessments must be funded.","Each named transfer and benefit is contingent on actual deed, insurer, beneficiary, trust, mortgage and legal documents; no tool can manufacture these records."],"tasks":["Prepare an unambiguous asset-title-beneficiary-creditor inventory with evidence status and unanswered legal questions.","Compute death-coverage gap from mortgage, support, education, final costs less available resources.","Compute real present value of net household support for 20 years at 3%; explain overlap with needs allocation.","Analyze disability elimination-period cash gap and benefits after waiting, testing $6k reserves.","Contrast term and permanent policy economics on identical premium budget and coverage need.","Estimate future value of investing $215 monthly for 20 years at 6%/12; disclose risk and taxes.","Compute first-year and five-year policy debt for $15k at 7%; net death-benefit implications.","Compute $30k at 8% for five years and compare with stressed $42k surrender value.","Calculate gross, loan, net cash and ordinary taxable gain in policy surrender illustration.","Compare policy owner, insured, premium payor, trustee, beneficiary and successor holder of powers.","Distinguish an ILIT-owned policy from one naming an ILIT as beneficiary, and discuss IRC §2035.","Calculate net house equity after mortgage and hypothetical 6% selling expenses.","Compare recorded title, survivorship, tenancy-in-common and trust-funding proof requirements.","Analyze conditional Garn–St. Germain due-on-sale protection for certain living-trust transfers.","Explain Connecticut probate workflow and likely CT-706 NT filing independent of tax bill.","State 2026 federal and Connecticut estate tax basic exclusions and how previous gifts affect use.","Compute the impact of a $29k qualifying gift under the 2026 $19k annual exclusion.","Calculate inherited date-of-death basis vs lifetime gift carryover basis sale gain.","Calculate two-year property carrying reserve and shortfall for a child beneficiary.","Provide a 1,500–2,000 word memorandum contrasting will + beneficiary designations, funded revocable trust and specialist irrevocable trust, including disability planning, tax reporting, funding, advisors and measurable success criteria."],"answers":["Coverage need $220,000+$250,000+$30,000+$10,000−$40,000=$470,000.","Support present value $30k×[1−(1.03)^−20]/.03≈$446,325; do not double count with $250k support target.","Disability 3-month essential wait gap $2,700×3=$8,100; $6k cash reserve leaves $2,100 initial shortfall; after $1,900 benefit, recurring essential gap $800/month.","Permanent vs term premium difference $250−$35=$215/month; invested $215×[(1+.06/12)^240−1]/(.06/12)≈$99,300 before taxes, fees, volatility.","Loan at 7%: year one simple interest $1,050; year five capitalized loan $15,000×1.07^5≈$21,038.","Loan at 8%: $30,000×1.08^5≈$44,080; exceeds $42,000 stressed surrender value by ≈$2,080.","Surrender $60k gross−$35k loan satisfaction=$25k net cash; basis $40k gives illustrative $20k gain and ~$4,400 federal tax at stable 22%, subject to actual contract rules.","$300k house−$200k mortgage=$100k equity before costs; sale cost at 6%=$18k; net proceeds ~$82k before other estate items.","2026 basic exclusions $15m federal and $15m CT; do not infer exemption from Connecticut Probate Court tax-return filing.","2026 $29k present-interest gift−$19k annual exclusion leaves $10k potentially reportable, usually using remaining lifetime exclusion.","Capital asset sold for $310k: inherited adjusted basis $300k means gain $10k; lifetime gift carryover $150k for gain means $160k gain before costs, assuming qualifying rules.","Property annual tax/insurance $4,800+maintenance $3,000+utilities $1,800=$9,600; two years $19,200 less $8,000 liquid reserves=$11,200 shortfall.","Trust beneficiary designation alone does not make trust owner. Mortgaged-home deed change does not forgive $200k debt. Federal due-on-sale protection is conditional and local counsel should review.","Life death proceeds generally may be federal-income-tax excluded for beneficiary but included in insured's taxable estate due to retained incidents of ownership; transferred policy may face three-year rule.","Professional memo avoids claiming a tax saving, basis reset or loan access without actual policy/deed/trust records; it preserves dependent, emergency and housing liquidity."],"rubric":["Insurance sizing and human-capital modeling with no double counting (20%)","Cash-value/loan stress and taxable policy outcomes (20%)","Deed, probate, funded-trust and beneficiary legal reasoning (20%)","Federal/CT gift, estate and basis analysis with valid dates (20%)","Generational succession, liquidity, implementation and ethical evidence (20%)"],"sources":["https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i706","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/individual-income-tax-portal/estate-and-gift-taxes/tax-information","https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/frequently-asked-questions-on-gift-taxes","https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rr-23-02.pdf","https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/life-insurance-illustrations","https://www.ctprobate.gov/trusts-estates","https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-118/t12/s1701j-3/","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"]}};
